WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.780
As the 2020 elections loom, the education
debates will be shaped by opinions that

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.920
get less attention than they deserve.
First: While most Americans give their

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.600
own community's schools an A or a B, just
a quarter give the nation's schools those grades.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.680
That's why voters tune out candidates

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.779
who suggest their local schools are
failing, even while accepting that

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.820
schools somewhere else may need stronger
medicine. Second: three-quarters of Americans

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.260
support teacher pay increases,
but support drops substantially after

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.379
respondents are told what the average
teacher actually earns. Teacher pay

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:46.700
increases have real appeal, but teachers
should take care not to overstate their case.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.920
Third: Americans — regardless of race — support
stricter school discipline by nearly

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.510
a ten to one margin. Parents will be
skeptical of proposals thought to weaken

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:02.010
school discipline. Candidates should take
care to listen to what voters think and

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.920
not just what advocates might have them believe.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.840
Do you think teachers deserve a raise?
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.600
what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.560
sure to LIKE and subscribe more research
and videos from AEI.

